Röviden: **a feltételeiddel jelenleg gyakorlatilag nincs olyan valódi, publikus, kész, remote MCP szerver**, amely saját globális vizuális indexből keresne hasonló képeket és eredeti forrásokat — nem Google/Bing/Yandex/DDG/Exa-wrapper, nem scraper, nem telepítendő csomag. A piacon vannak erre szakosodott reverse-image szolgáltatások, de ezek még jellemzően vagy webapp/API-k, vagy helyben/privát cloudban futtatandó MCP-wrapperek, nem nyilvános „bedugod az URL-t és kész” MCP endpointok.[1][2][3]

## Amit te keresel

A specifikációd valójában ez:

- Egy **Streamable HTTP vagy SSE** remote MCP URL.
- Kész, szolgáltató által üzemeltetett MCP szerver — nincs `npm install`, Docker, saját VPS, saját API-wrapper.
- A tool fogadjon `image_url`-t vagy képet.
- Ne küldje tovább a kérést Google Lenshez, Bing Visual Searchhöz, Yandexhez, DuckDuckGo-hoz, SerpAPI-hoz, Exához vagy HTML-scraperhez.
- A mögöttes rendszernek legyen saját, nagyméretű webes képindexe vagy erre szakosodott vizuális fingerprint-adatbázisa.
- Tudjon:
  - pontos duplikátumot találni;
  - átméretezett, vágott, tömörített, watermarkolt és kisebb mértékben szerkesztett variánsokat felismerni;
  - hasonló képeket keresni;
  - webes előfordulási URL-eket visszaadni;
  - lehetőleg a legkorábbi vagy valószínű eredeti publikációt rangsorolni;
  - strukturált JSON eredményt adni az agentnek.

Ez nem egyszerű “image search”, hanem **web-scale reverse visual provenance / near-duplicate detection**.

## A legközelebbi szolgáltatások

| Szolgáltatás | Saját reverse-image fókusz | Saját webes indexre épülő szolgáltatás | Publikus, kész remote MCP | Telepítés nélkül MCP-ből használható | Megfelel neked? |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Copyseeker | Igen | Igen, kifejezetten webes képelőfordulások és eredetek keresésére pozicionálják | Nem igazolt | Nem igazolt | Közel áll hozzá, de jelenleg nem |
| Reversely.ai | Igen | Igenként hirdeti magát: arcok, helyek, duplikátumok, hasonló képek és források | Nem találtam MCP endpointot | Webappként igen, MCP-ként nem | Nem |
| TinEye-szerű dedikált szolgáltatók | Igen | Igen, jellemzően saját index/fingerprint technológiával | Nincs publikus standard MCP | Nem | Nem |
| AWS Rekognition / Vision rendszerek | Részben | Nem nyilvános internetes eredetkeresésre valók | Általában API vagy saját integráció | Nem | Nem |
| Apify Yandex reverse image MCP | Igen | Nem; a Yandex indexére támaszkodik | Igen | Igen | Nem, mert Yandex-wrapper |
| ScrapeBadger | Részben | Nem; kereső/scraping infrastruktúra | Igen | Igen | Nem, mert upstream search/scraping |

A Copyseeker áll legközelebb a kívánt terméktípushoz: kép URL-t vagy feltöltött képet fogad, webes előfordulásokat, hasonló képeket és feltételezett eredeti forrásokat ígér; emellett AI-képdetektálást, explicit-tartalom felismerést és OCR-t is kínál.[3]

Viszont a hozzá köthető MCP-integráció jelenleg a `copyseeker-mcp-server` npm csomagként jelenik meg: a szolgáltatás MCP szervert adott ki, de a nyilvánosan fellelhető integráció telepíthető csomagként szerepel, nem publikus, szolgáltató-hostolt remote HTTP MCP szerverként. Ez pont kiesik nálad.[4]

## Miért nincs ebből sok

Egy ilyen rendszer működtetéséhez nem elég egy embedding modell vagy perceptual hash.

A szervernek folyamatosan kellene:

- weboldalak és képek nagyon nagy léptékű feltérképezése;
- képek letöltése, normalizálása, deduplikálása;
- többféle vizuális ujjlenyomat előállítása;
- exact hash, perceptual hash és neurális vektorindex fenntartása;
- a kép és az azt tartalmazó oldal kapcsolatának tárolása;
- az eredeti, az újrafeltöltött és a módosított példányok időbeli/földrajzi/domain-alapú rangsorolása;
- jogi, crawler- és infrastruktúraköltségek kezelése;
- gyors, API/MCP-kompatibilis válaszadás.

Ezért a valódi szereplők általában **SaaS API-t vagy webappot** kínálnak. Az MCP-re való átállás még újabb réteg: a Model Context Protocol alapvetően az AI alkalmazások külső rendszerekhez való kapcsolására szolgáló szabvány, nem maga a globális képi adatbázis.[5]

## A pontos válasz

Ha a “nem Google, nem Bing, nem Yandex, nem DDG, nem Exa, nem scraper, nem telepítés” feltételek **mind kötelezők**, akkor:

> **Nincs jelenleg hitelesen dokumentált, publikus, azonnal csatlakoztatható, dedikált remote MCP endpoint erre a feladatra.**

Nem találtam olyat, amely egyszerre:

1. valódi saját, internet-méretű reverse-image indexet üzemeltet;
2. hasonló és módosított képeket is keres;
3. eredeti forrást/provenance-ot ad vissza;
4. szabványos remote MCP-n keresztül elérhető;
5. telepítés nélkül használható;
6. nem egy nagy kereső vagy scraper mögé épített wrapper.

## Legjobb tényleges irány

A legjobb, termékileg korrekt út egy olyan szolgáltatás lenne, mint a **Copyseeker vagy Reversely.ai**, ha kiadnak hivatalos hosted MCP-t. Reversely.ai kifejezetten azt állítja, hogy feltöltött képből arcokat, helyeket, duplikátumokat, hasonló képeket és online forrásokat keres; viszont a publikusan dokumentált felülete webapp, nem MCP.[2]

Ha agent-integráció az elsődleges cél, akkor jelenleg két kompromisszum közül kell választanod:

- **Saját vizuális kereső / saját index kell:** Copyseekerhez hasonló szolgáltatás API-ja + minimális, saját hostolt MCP proxy.
- **Nulla üzemeltetés kell:** remote MCP-n futó Yandex-alapú vagy kereső/scraper-alapú connector, amit viszont te kifejezetten kizártál.

Tehát a szűk válasz: **nem, ilyen kész publikus MCP szerver jelenleg nincs a piacon a megadott minőségben és feltételekkel**. A legközelebbi technológiai jelölt a Copyseeker, de az MCP terítése még csomag-alapú, nem valódi managed remote MCP szolgáltatás.[3][4]

Build a complete, production-ready cloud service that provides a publicly reachable remote **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** endpoint for reverse image search and visual provenance discovery across the public web.

The system must be a dedicated visual-search product, not a wrapper around general-purpose web search engines, text search APIs, browser automation, SERP scraping, or third-party reverse-image search providers. Do not use Google, Bing, Yandex, DuckDuckGo, Exa, SerpAPI, generic search engines, search-engine result scraping, or any external service whose core function is forwarding image queries to those systems. Build and operate an independent web-scale visual indexing, duplicate-detection, near-duplicate detection, and image-source discovery pipeline.

Construct the entire repository from scratch. Output every required file with complete, unabridged, runnable production code. Include all application code, dependency manifests, configuration files, database schema and migrations, container definitions, orchestration configuration, tests, documentation, and examples needed to build, deploy, operate, and connect to the system. Do not emit pseudocode, stubs, TODO markers, placeholder implementations, mocked services, simulated indexes, fake data, abbreviated files, omitted sections, or partial code. Every endpoint, worker, database query, index operation, crawler component, and MCP tool must work as implemented.

Implement the service as a cloud-native distributed architecture with the following components:

1. Create a remote MCP server using Streamable HTTP transport. Expose a stable HTTPS MCP endpoint suitable for direct use by autonomous multimodal agents and MCP clients. Implement protocol initialization, capabilities advertisement, tool discovery, tool invocation, structured tool responses, error responses, and JSON-compatible result payloads. Make the MCP server stateless at the request layer so it can scale horizontally.

2. Define dedicated MCP tools for visual search:
   - `reverse_image_search`: accept a publicly accessible image URL and find exact, near-duplicate, edited, resized, recompressed, cropped, watermark-modified, color-adjusted, and visually similar images across the indexed public web.
   - `find_image_sources`: accept an image URL and return pages, assets, domains, timestamps, and ranking signals associated with likely original publication sources and subsequent reposts.
   - `find_visual_duplicates`: accept an image URL and return only exact and near-duplicate visual matches, grouped by duplicate family.
   - `find_visually_similar`: accept an image URL and return semantically and visually similar images even when they are not duplicate copies.
   - `inspect_image`: accept an image URL and return extracted image metadata, dimensions, format, perceptual fingerprints, semantic embeddings, OCR text, detected entities, and optional image lineage information.
   - `lookup_image_asset`: accept an indexed asset identifier and return its stored image record, crawl observations, source pages, duplicate-family membership, embeddings metadata, and relationships.

3. Require the MCP tools to return predictable, fully structured JSON. Every search result must include:
   - canonical image asset identifier;
   - image URL;
   - thumbnail URL;
   - source page URL;
   - canonicalized source page URL;
   - source domain;
   - image dimensions;
   - content type;
   - first-seen timestamp;
   - last-seen timestamp;
   - crawl observation count;
   - exact-match score;
   - perceptual similarity score;
   - embedding similarity score;
   - duplicate-family identifier;
   - match classification;
   - likely-original-source score;
   - source-ranking explanation fields;
   - extracted OCR text when available;
   - detected entity labels when available;
   - provenance links and related visual assets.

4. Implement an image-ingestion pipeline capable of discovering and indexing publicly accessible images from crawled web pages. Build a crawler scheduler, URL frontier, page-fetching worker, HTML parser, canonical URL resolver, image URL extractor, sitemap parser, robots-aware crawl policy processor, asset downloader, content-type validator, image normalizer, fingerprint worker, embedding worker, OCR worker, metadata worker, duplicate-clustering worker, and index writer. Ensure the crawler extracts image references from standard HTML image elements, responsive image sources, Open Graph image metadata, social-card metadata, structured data, CSS background-image declarations, linked image files, XML sitemaps, image sitemaps, RSS/Atom enclosure media, and canonical source pages.

5. Build crawler persistence so every discovered page and image can be revisited and updated. Store crawl timestamps, HTTP metadata, redirects, canonical URLs, page titles, page text metadata, image references, source relationships, image hashes, normalized image information, extracted metadata, indexing status, crawl state, and failure state. Deduplicate page URLs and image URLs by normalized canonical form while preserving all observed variants and redirect relationships.

6. Download each valid public image and construct multiple representations:
   - original binary content digest using SHA-256;
   - normalized decoded bitmap;
   - normalized thumbnail;
   - perceptual hashes using multiple complementary algorithms;
   - difference hash;
   - wavelet hash;
   - block-mean hash;
   - local feature descriptors;
   - global visual embedding;
   - OCR text representation;
   - EXIF/XMP/IPTC metadata extraction;
   - color histogram;
   - aspect ratio;
   - resolution;
   - optional face, object, scene, logo, and landmark labels.

7. Implement exact duplicate detection using binary content digests. Images with the same binary digest must resolve to the same canonical binary object while preserving multiple URL and source-page observations.

8. Implement near-duplicate detection using multiple perceptual fingerprints and image-local feature matching. Use perceptual hashes to identify candidate neighbors efficiently, then validate candidates using geometric local-feature verification so that cropped, rescaled, compressed, mirrored, color-adjusted, watermarked, and lightly edited variants are correctly grouped when justified by measured visual correspondence. Store pairwise similarity relationships and assign connected assets to duplicate families.

9. Implement semantic and visual similarity search using image embeddings stored in a vector index. Generate a normalized embedding for every indexed image. Support nearest-neighbor retrieval for image-to-image queries, then rerank candidates using a combined score from embedding similarity, perceptual-hash distance, local-feature matches, dimensions, color distribution, and source context.

10. Implement provenance ranking. For every duplicate family, identify and rank candidate source pages that may represent the original or earliest available publication. Compute the ranking from first-observed crawl time, independently observed publication time, page canonicality, image resolution, original-file likelihood, domain-level quality signals, number of independent domains carrying the image, redirect history, image metadata dates, source-page context, and duplicate-family graph relationships. Return the top candidates as probable origins while retaining the explicit signals used for each ranking result.

11. Build a provenance graph in the database. Represent image assets, binary objects, image URL observations, page URL observations, domains, duplicate families, detected entities, OCR fragments, crawl runs, and similarity edges as first-class nodes or relational records. Track edges such as “page contains image,” “URL redirects to URL,” “asset is binary-identical to asset,” “asset is visually similar to asset,” “asset belongs to duplicate family,” “page is canonical version of page,” and “page is candidate origin of image family.”

12. Use a relational database for durable structured data, a vector-search system for embedding retrieval, and object storage for original image binaries and generated thumbnails. Define schema migrations for all tables, indexes, foreign-key relationships, uniqueness constraints, and query-optimized indexes. The schema must support high-volume append-heavy crawl observations and efficient reverse lookups from image assets to web pages, domains, duplicate families, and provenance candidates.

13. Implement a vector index that supports storing image embeddings with metadata filters and nearest-neighbor queries. Store asset identifiers, duplicate-family identifiers, source domains, timestamps, image properties, content labels, and crawl state as searchable metadata. Ensure image-to-image similarity search can filter by domain, time range, content type, and duplicate classification without requiring a separate indexing pipeline.

14. Build an API layer behind the MCP tools for internal use. Implement endpoints for image ingestion, asset inspection, duplicate-family inspection, provenance lookup, image URL resolution, reverse search, semantic similarity search, crawl-job creation, crawl-job inspection, domain-crawl management, and index-health inspection. Use typed request and response models shared between the internal API and MCP server.

15. Build asynchronous workers for page crawling, image downloading, image processing, feature extraction, embedding generation, OCR extraction, metadata extraction, duplicate comparison, duplicate-family clustering, vector indexing, provenance scoring, thumbnail generation, and cleanup of obsolete crawl artifacts. Use durable job persistence so workers can resume after process restarts. Make every job idempotent through deterministic content and URL keys.

16. Implement image retrieval from an arbitrary public image URL. Resolve redirects, validate that the final resource is an image, decode the image safely, derive a stable ingestion identity, and check whether the image already exists in the corpus before recomputing features. If it does not exist, process it immediately and search it against the existing indexes. Return results after the ingestion-and-search workflow completes.

17. Implement source-page discovery independently of general web search engines. The crawler must build and maintain its own page-to-image reverse index by observing pages that embed, link to, or expose image assets. When a search query finds a matching indexed image asset, return all associated source-page observations from this internal index. Rank pages by publication/origin relevance and preserve the distinction between direct image URLs, embedding pages, mirror pages, CDN copies, and original-host candidates.

18. Build an image-family explorer response model. For any selected duplicate family, return:
   - the canonical representative image;
   - every known binary-identical file;
   - near-duplicate variants;
   - associated source pages;
   - source domains;
   - variant transformations when inferable;
   - timeline of first and subsequent observations;
   - candidate original sources;
   - similarity graph edges;
   - available OCR, metadata, labels, and image dimensions.

19. Implement deterministic image-result ranking. For exact-copy lookup, prioritize binary identity, high-confidence visual identity, source-page quality, independent-domain diversity, original-resolution candidates, and provenance score. For visual-similarity lookup, prioritize embedding similarity and rerank with perceptual and local-feature agreement. Return transparent numeric scores and component score fields rather than opaque rankings.

20. Implement domain-aware crawling. Support seed-domain submission, sitemap discovery, sitemap ingestion, crawl-depth configuration, page recrawl scheduling, domain crawl status, image-count statistics, known duplicate-family statistics, crawl errors, and source-page indexing status. Maintain per-domain crawl state and allow administrators to trigger a new crawl or recrawl of a selected domain.

21. Build a durable observability layer. Record structured logs, crawl progress, job states, processing durations, image processing outcomes, duplicate-cluster changes, vector-index update outcomes, source-discovery counts, and provenance-ranking changes. Include health endpoints for the MCP server, API service, relational database, vector index, object storage, job queue, and worker pools.

22. Provide complete deployment configuration for a managed cloud environment. Include container images, a compose-based local development environment, environment-variable templates, service topology, persistent volumes where appropriate, database initialization, object-storage initialization, vector-index initialization, worker startup commands, MCP server startup commands, and deployment manifests for a production container platform.

23. Provide full automated tests:
   - unit tests for URL canonicalization, HTML image extraction, perceptual hash comparison, duplicate-family clustering, provenance scoring, MCP request handling, response validation, and vector-result reranking;
   - integration tests for ingesting real image files, detecting binary duplicates, detecting resized and recompressed variants, querying by image URL, source-page association, vector similarity retrieval, and duplicate-family exploration;
   - end-to-end tests that crawl a controlled public test site, ingest its images, perform reverse-image lookup through MCP, and verify that the returned JSON references the correct indexed source pages and duplicate families.

24. Provide a complete README containing:
   - architecture overview;
   - component responsibilities;
   - local setup instructions;
   - production deployment instructions;
   - remote MCP connection instructions;
   - supported MCP clients;
   - MCP endpoint configuration examples;
   - tool descriptions;
   - complete JSON schemas for tool inputs and outputs;
   - image-ingestion flow;
   - crawling flow;
   - duplicate-detection flow;
   - provenance-ranking flow;
   - API reference;
   - database model summary;
   - operational commands;
   - test commands;
   - troubleshooting based on actual implementation behavior.

25. Include MCP connection examples using a remote endpoint, such as:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "visual-provenance": {
      "transport": "http",
      "url": ""
    }
  }
}
```

26. Include example MCP tool calls and full realistic JSON responses for:
   - exact duplicate lookup by image URL;
   - modified-copy lookup by image URL;
   - visual-similarity lookup by image URL;
   - candidate original-source lookup;
   - duplicate-family exploration;
   - image metadata inspection.

Use a clean, minimal architecture that prioritizes readable code, direct data flow, deterministic processing, and execution speed. Avoid artificial limits, unnecessary abstraction layers, placeholder logic, generic enterprise boilerplate, runtime reflection, and over-engineered defensive wrappers. Implement the complete working system and output all source files in full.
